<p class="lede">Testing is part of the language and the command: a <code>test</code> block holds the check, <code>assert</code> makes it, and <code>bayan test</code> runs each test in its own process. The chapter takes <code>grade</code> from a first passing test to coverage.</p>

<h2 id="first">The function and its first test</h2>
<p>It turns a score into a letter, failing on a score that cannot exist; the error type has two cases.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error GradeError {
    negative
    tooHigh
}

string grade(i32 score) fails GradeError {
    fail GradeError.negative if score &lt; 0
    fail GradeError.tooHigh if score &gt; 100
    return "A" if score &gt;= 90
    return "B" if score &gt;= 80
    return "C"
}</code></pre>
<p>A test is a <code>test</code> block with a name in quotes, in the same file as the code. Add this below <code>grade</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "ninety is an A" {
    assert (try grade(90)) == "A"
}</code></pre>
<p><code>grade</code> can fail, so calling it needs <code>try</code>, as anywhere else. The parentheses around <code>try grade(90)</code> matter; the next section shows why.</p>
<p><code>bayan run</code> ignores tests, <code>bayan test</code> ignores the program. The complete file below has this chapter's tests, a <code>print</code>, and one <code>bayan run</code> output:</p>
<example name="book-12-grade"/>
<p>Test it:</p>
<pre><code>bayan test grade.byn</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">running 5 tests in 1 module

◆ 5 passed · 0.26s</code></pre>
<p>Five, not three: the last test is a table, and each of its three rows counts. The exit code is 0 when every test passes.</p>

<h2 id="assert">assert</h2>
<p><code>assert</code> is a statement, not a function: <code>assert x == 1</code>, parentheses allowed but not needed. There is no assertion library; the comparison is plain code. In return you get the failure message. Change <code>&gt;=</code> to <code>&gt;</code> in <code>grade</code> so 90 is no longer an A, and run again:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">running 5 tests in 1 module

  ✗ grade: ninety is an A
      assert (try grade(90)) == "A"
              │              │
              │              false
              B
      grade.byn:17

◆ 4 passed · ✗ 1 failed · 0 pending · 0.17s
  rerun the failure: bayan test --only "ninety is an A"</code></pre>
<p>Read the tree bottom-up: the comparison is <code>false</code> and its left side, <code>try grade(90)</code>, produced <code>B</code>. Every evaluated sub-expression shows its value, so the cause is visible without a <code>print</code>. The last line reruns that test; a failure exits 3.</p>
<p>The name before each test is its module, here <code>grade</code>, then the test's name. Make it say what is checked: "negative scores fail" reads well in a failure list, "test2" does not.</p>

<h2 id="fallible">Testing code that fails</h2>
<p>A fallible function on the happy path needs <code>try</code>. Two mistakes are easy, each fixed by the message. First, no <code>try</code> at all:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "ninety is an A" {
    assert grade(90) == "A"
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `grade` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
  --&gt; grade.byn:17:12

  17 │     assert grade(90) == "A"
     │            ^^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`
     │ 17 │     assert try grade(90) == "A"
     │    │            ++++</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>grade.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Half right: put <code>try</code> in front of the whole comparison and the compiler objects, because the comparison itself cannot fail:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1054:</span> `try` covers `grade(90) == "A"`, which cannot fail
  --&gt; grade.byn:17:16

  17 │     assert try grade(90) == "A"
     │                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ cannot fail
     │
  help: wrap the part that fails in parentheses: `(try grade(90))`
     │ 17 │     assert (try grade(90)) == "A"
     │    │            ++++++++++++++</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>grade.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>For the <em>error</em> path you write no <code>catch</code>: <code>assert f() fails E.case</code> passes when the call fails with exactly that case, and <code>assert f() fails E</code> with any case of <code>E</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "negative scores fail" {
    assert grade(-1) fails GradeError.negative
}</code></pre>
<p>When the call fails with another case, or does not fail, the test fails and says which. These two come from the compiler's own tests, so the module name has a <code>run_</code> prefix:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">  ✗ run_assert-fails.test: wrong case
      assert check(11) fails E.bad
             │
             failed with E.worse
      assert-fails.test.byn:25

  ✗ run_assert-fails.test: does not fail
      assert check(3) fails E
             │
             returned 3
      assert-fails.test.byn:29</code></pre>
<p>The call must be able to fail: <code>fails</code> on a function that cannot fail is rejected at compile time with BYN2605, <code>`assert … fails` needs a call that can fail</code>.</p>

<h2 id="cases">One test, many inputs</h2>
<p>A table is a test with parameters and an <code>@cases</code> attribute above it. Each row is a tuple, or a single value for one parameter, and every row runs as its own test.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases((100, "A"), (89, "B"), (79, "C"))
test "grade table" (i32 score, string expected) {
    assert (try grade(score)) == expected
}</code></pre>
<p>With <code>-v</code> passing tests are listed, each row with its values:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test -v">running 5 tests in 1 module
  ◆ grade: ninety is an A
  ◆ grade: negative scores fail
  ◆ grade: grade table (score: 100, expected: "A")
  ◆ grade: grade table (score: 89, expected: "B")
  ◆ grade: grade table (score: 79, expected: "C")

◆ 5 passed · 0.07s</code></pre>
<p>A failing row names itself the same way, for example <code>quantity bounds (input: 101, valid: true)</code>, and the rerun hint carries it. Values must be constants matching their parameter's type; a mismatch is a compile error:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases((1, "yes"), (2, true))
test "flags" (i32 n, bool ok) {
    assert(ok)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `bool`, found `string`

  1 │ @cases((1, "yes"), (2, true))
    │            ^^^^^</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler, from <code>tests/cases/diag/cases-type-mismatch.test</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. <code>@cases(someConstList)</code> takes its rows from a constant list; today it is error BYN2774, "cases from a const list are not supported yet". Write each row out.</note>

<h2 id="where">Where tests live</h2>
<p>Tests sit beside the code, as above, or in a file ending in <code>.test.byn</code>: a sibling <code>grade.test.byn</code> belongs to the same module, sees <code>grade</code> and <code>GradeError</code> with no import, and keeps a module's own file free of test code:</p>
<pre><code>grading/
  grade.byn           union error GradeError and string grade(…)
  grade.test.byn      the tests</code></pre>
<p>Give <code>bayan test</code> the directory:</p>
<pre><code>bayan test .</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">running 2 tests in 1 module

◆ 2 passed · 0.06s</code></pre>
<p>In a project (chapter 11), <code>bayan test</code> with no path runs every module's tests: sibling test files in <code>src/</code> and the files in <code>tests/</code>, which join the entry module and need their own imports. A project made by <code>bayan new</code> already has one passing test, so a fresh project passes with no edits.</p>
<note>The specification says tests in <code>tests/</code> see only a module's public API and tests beside the code can read private members; today a sibling test cannot read one either (BYN2005). Test through the public methods.</note>

<h2 id="running">Running and filtering</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>bayan test</code></td><td>runs the tests of the project in the current directory</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>bayan test file.byn</code> or <code>bayan test dir</code></td><td>runs the tests of that file or directory</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>-v</code></td><td>also lists passing and pending tests</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--only "text"</code></td><td>runs only tests whose name contains the text; repeat the flag for more</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--changed</code></td><td>only modules with a file that differs from <code>HEAD</code> (<code>--base ref</code> compares with another ref)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--mode debug|safe|fast</code></td><td>the build mode for the test program</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>prints the results as JSON instead of text</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--coverage</code>, <code>--mutate</code></td><td>the last two sections</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The first, failing line of a failure is also the command to repeat it, so you never retype a name; the exit code tells a script what happened. In a project, <code>bayan test</code> also writes the JSON to <code>out/test/results.json</code>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>every test passed</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>the program or a test did not compile</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>a test failed, or coverage or mutation score is under its minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>toolchain problem (no C compiler, unwritable cache)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3 id="output">Output, pending and failures</h3>
<p>A passing test's output is hidden unless <code>-v</code>; a failing test always shows it under its name. A test not written yet calls <code>todo("reason")</code> and reports as <em>pending</em>, never passing, so a plan cannot hide in a green run. Take these two tests, next to <code>grade</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "wip" {
    todo("cover tooHigh")
}

test "chatty" {
    print("checking five")
    assert (try grade(5)) == "B"
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">running 2 tests in 1 module

  ✗ grading: chatty
      checking five
      assert (try grade(5)) == "B"
              │             │
              │             false
              C
      todo.test.byn:7

◆ 0 passed · ✗ 1 failed · 1 pending · 0.49s
  rerun the failure: bayan test --only "chatty"</code></pre>
<p>The printed line comes above the assertion; <code>wip</code> is pending.</p>
<p>Each test runs in its own process: a crash is one failed test, not a dead run.</p>

<h2 id="coverage">Coverage</h2>
<p>Passing tests do not say what they touched. <code>bayan test --coverage</code> counts <strong>lines</strong> that ran, <strong>branches</strong> (each side of a condition) taken both ways, and <strong>error paths</strong>: <code>fails</code> cases some test made the function return. Run it on <code>grade</code> with only <code>ninety is an A</code> and <code>negative scores fail</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test --coverage">running 2 tests in 1 module

◆ 2 passed · 0.25s
coverage: lines 3/5 (60%), branches 4/8 (50%), error paths 1/2 (50%)
  grade.byn: lines 3/5, branches 4/8, error paths 1/2
uncovered branch: grade.byn:8:32 never true
uncovered branch: grade.byn:9:19 never false
uncovered branch: grade.byn:10:19 never true
uncovered branch: grade.byn:10:19 never false
uncovered error path: grade.byn: grade GradeError.tooHigh</code></pre>
<p>Line 8, column 32 is <code>score &gt; 100</code>, which no test made true: the <code>tooHigh</code> error path. Lines 9 and 10 were seen one way only, because only 90 reached them. Tests with 101, 85 and 70 take all three counts to 100%. Paths are shortened here; the compiler prints the path it was given.</p>
<p>Without <code>--coverage</code>, <code>bayan test --mode debug</code> prints the one-line summary after the pass count; <code>--coverage</code> also writes <code>out/test/bayan-quality.json</code> for tools.</p>
<p>To gate coverage, set minimums in <code>package.byn</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Package {
    name: "local/gate",
    version: "0.1.0",
    edition: 2027,
    kind: .lib,
    capabilities: [],
    deps: [],
    test: Tests { lines: 100, errorPaths: 100 },
    // …
}</code></pre>
<p>The gate runs with <code>bayan test --coverage</code>; under a minimum the run fails with exit code 3 and lists what to cover:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test --coverage">bayan: lines 60% &lt; 100%
uncovered line: src/gate/grade.byn:10
uncovered line: src/gate/grade.byn:11
bayan: error paths 50% &lt; 100%
uncovered error path: grade GradeError.tooHigh (src/gate/grade.byn)</code></pre>
<p>A test killed on timeout leaves its coverage unknown, not uncovered. <code>Tests</code> also has <code>branches</code>: the example sets two, and an omitted minimum is not enforced.</p>

<h2 id="mutation">Do the tests notice a change?</h2>
<p>Coverage says a line ran, not that a test would notice it being wrong. <code>bayan test --mutate</code> makes small changes one at a time (<code>&lt;</code> to <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>90</code> to <code>91</code>, an error case swapped) and runs the tests against each: a change a test fails on is <em>killed</em>, one nothing notices <em>survived</em>, one no test reaches <em>not reached</em>. The run ends:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test --mutate">grade.byn:7:33 condition `score &lt; 0` -&gt; `!(score &lt; 0)`: killed
grade.byn:7:33 boundary `score &lt; 0` -&gt; `score &lt;= 0`: survived
grade.byn:7:41 literal `0` -&gt; `1`: survived
…
grade.byn:10:28 literal `80` -&gt; `79`: not reached
11 killed, 6 survived, 0 timeout, 8 not reached, 0 marked, 0 not emitted</code></pre>
<p>The survivor at <code>score &lt; 0</code> to <code>score &lt;= 0</code> says no test tries a score of 0; a boundary test would kill it. Each line gives the position, the kind of change, the old and new text, and the result.</p>

<h2 id="planned">Planned test features</h2>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Each fragment below comes from the specification (§20). The compiler rejects them today: <code>@pairwise</code> is "unknown attribute", and <code>property</code>, <code>bench</code> and <code>snapshot</code> are not recognised.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">@pairwise
test "parses across currencies" (Currency c, bool negative) { … }

property "balances sum to zero" (Span&lt;Expense&gt; xs) { … }

bench "parse ledger" { measure Ledger.read(sample) }

@nightly
fuzz "reader never crashes" (string text) { … }</code></pre>
<p>These are the intended shapes: <code>@pairwise</code> runs a test over pairs of parameter values, not every combination; <code>property</code> generates inputs from the parameter types and shrinks a failure; <code>bench</code> reports time and allocations per operation; <code>fuzz</code> feeds random input, saving failures under <code>testdata/</code>. The same section describes tests that run <code>///</code> doc-comment code (not run today) and <code>snapshot(x)</code> with <code>bayan test --update</code>. For benchmarking now, see the <a href="cookbook-testing.html">testing cookbook</a>.</p>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>You write <code>assert try grade(90) == "A"</code> where <code>grade</code> fails. What does the compiler say?</summary>
<p>BYN1054, with <code>(try grade(90)) == "A"</code> as the fix; without <code>try</code>, BYN2602 (<a href="#fallible">details</a>).</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>The failure below is from a real run. What did <code>grade(90)</code> return?
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">  ✗ grade: ninety is an A
      assert (try grade(90)) == "A"
              │              │
              │              false
              B</code></pre></summary>
<p><code>"B"</code>, under <code>(try grade(90))</code>; an error would say <code>failed with</code>, and "A" is the expected value (<a href="#assert">details</a>).</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>A test body is only <code>todo("later")</code>. How does <code>bayan test</code> report it?</summary>
<p>Pending: in the summary and as <code>(pending)</code> under <code>-v</code>, never passed, and it does not fail the run (<a href="#output">details</a>).</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>The coverage report ends with <code>uncovered error path: grade GradeError.tooHigh</code>. What do you write?</summary>
<p><code>assert grade(101) fails GradeError.tooHigh</code>; error paths count by case, so 100 (which returns a letter) or a <code>negative</code> test will not do (<a href="#coverage">details</a>).</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary><code>package.byn</code> has <code>test: Tests { lines: 100 }</code>, and your tests cover 90% of lines. Which command fails, and with which exit code?</summary>
<p>Only <code>bayan test --coverage</code>, exit code 3; plain <code>bayan test</code> gives 0, as it does not check the gate (<a href="#coverage">details</a>).</p>
</details>

